-- allium: 3
-- 02-calculation-graph.allium

-- Scope: The content-addressed calculation graph: Veridian's memoization
--   system of record (extreme reuse of results).
-- Includes: model versions, requests pinned to a knowledge time, planned steps,
--   immutable nodes keyed by calc_id, memo hits, stampede control, early
--   cutoff, retries, timeouts and ringfenced failure.
-- Split (D-065): this file holds the core (model versions, requests, planned
--   steps, the calculation node). Node execution and request outcomes are
--   02b-node-execution, payload retention and recomputation
--   02c-payload-retention, attempt records and inspection
--   02d-attempts-and-inspection.
-- Excludes:
--   - Bitemporal input versions (01-bitemporal-data.allium).
--   - Identity, rights, audit (04-identity-access-audit.allium).
--   - Worker supervision and wire protocol (07); a worker is the external party
--     that fulfils DeterministicComputation.
--   - Instrument maths and FRTB (09, 11).
--   - Standing views that re-evaluate when new data arrives (later spec).
-- Dependencies: 01-bitemporal-data.allium, 05-configuration-release.allium.
--
-- Identity rule (the heart of memoization):
--   calc_id = hash(model version identity, as_of, content hashes of the
--                  resolved input versions, result_hash of each prerequisite
--                  node)
--   There is no "calculation context" in v1 (D-072, correcting D-003):
--   anything that must change identity enters through input versions.
--   The request's known_at chooses which versions are resolved but is NOT
--   hashed, so identical data reached at different times reuses results.
--   Hashing prerequisite result_hash (not prerequisite calc_id) gives early
--   cutoff: an upstream change that leaves a result byte-identical leaves
--   every downstream calc_id unchanged.
-- Complete nodes are never invalidated: a node is valid forever for exactly
-- its inputs. "Invalidation" is a new request resolving to new calc_ids.

use "./01-bitemporal-data.allium" as data
use "./05-configuration-release.allium" as cfg
use "./02b-node-execution.allium" as exec
use "./02c-payload-retention.allium" as ret
use "./02d-attempts-and-inspection.allium" as insp

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract DeterministicComputation {
    compute: (calc_id: String, model: ModelVersion, as_of: Timestamp, inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>, prerequisite_results: Set<String>) -> String

    @invariant Purity
        -- The result depends only on the arguments: no clock, no environment
        -- variables, no direct database or network reads, no global state.

    @invariant Determinism
        -- Identical arguments produce a byte-identical result and therefore
        -- the same result_hash, on any worker, after any restart.

    @invariant CanonicalInputOrdering
        -- Inputs and prerequisite results are consumed in a canonical order
        -- (sorted by content hash); set iteration order never affects output.

    @invariant CanonicalRounding
        -- result_hash is computed over the canonical form of the result
        -- (D-004, D-015): rounding half-even; monetary amounts to 4 decimal
        -- places of the currency unit; rates and percentages to one hundredth
        -- of a basis point (0.000001 as a decimal rate); discount factors to
        -- 7 significant digits. Year fractions are exact rationals defined by their
        -- day-count convention (ISDA 2006 s4.16) and are never rounded.
        -- Timestamps are UTC with microsecond precision. Two results equal
        -- after canonical rounding have the same result_hash, which drives
        -- early cutoff.

    @invariant VersionedHashScheme
        -- Every calc_id, content_hash and result_hash names the hashing
        -- scheme that produced it (algorithm and canonical encoding version,
        -- D-041). A new scheme arrives only in a reviewed release; existing
        -- identifiers stay valid under their own scheme and are never
        -- rewritten. A node is carried into a new scheme only by recomputing
        -- it and checking that the result is equal after canonical rounding;
        -- the link between the old and new identifiers is recorded.

    @guidance
        -- Random seeds, where needed, are derived from calc_id.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A versioned calculation function (e.g. "irs_pv" 1.2.0) bound to exact code.
-- It arrives only inside a configuration release (05, D-016): four-eyes is
-- the release's code review. Identity is the code, not the release, so a model
-- carried unchanged into the next release keeps its calc_ids.
entity ModelVersion {
    function_name: String
    version: String
    code_hash: String
    first_release: cfg/ConfigurationRelease
    registered_at: Timestamp
}

-- What a user asked for, pinned to the moment it was asked.
entity CalculationRequest {
    requested_by: data/User
    model: ModelVersion
    subjects: Set<data/InputSeries>
    -- Versions read as they are besides the subjects (platform requests, 26).
    pinned_inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>
    data_class: data/DataClass
    as_of: Timestamp
    known_at: Timestamp
    -- Who or what asked: a screen, API client, batch run (06); free text, used
    -- for traceability and for batch runs to find their request.
    origin_ref: String?
    requested_at: Timestamp
    status: open | fulfilled | unfulfilled
    completed_at: Timestamp when status = fulfilled | unfulfilled
    failure_reason: String when status = unfulfilled

    transitions status {
        open -> fulfilled
        open -> unfulfilled
        terminal: fulfilled, unfulfilled
    }

    -- True once the planning step's DAG has been turned into evaluation steps.
    plan_expanded: Boolean

    steps: PlannedStep with request = this
    all_done: plan_expanded and steps.all(s => s.status = done)
    any_abandoned: steps.any(s => s.status = abandoned)
}

-- One node of the request's plan. It can only be identified (given a calc_id)
-- once all prerequisites are done, because calc_id includes their result hashes.
entity PlannedStep {
    request: CalculationRequest
    model: ModelVersion
    inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>
    prerequisites: Set<PlannedStep>
    -- planning: the Julia planner computing the DAG (D-020);
    -- evaluation: any node of that DAG (pricing, shocks, sensitivities, ...).
    purpose: planning | evaluation
    status: waiting | running | done | abandoned | cancelled

    calc_id: String when status = running | done | abandoned
    node: CalculationNode when status = running | done | abandoned
    memo_hit: Boolean when status = running | done | abandoned

    transitions status {
        waiting -> running
        waiting -> done
        waiting -> cancelled
        waiting -> abandoned
        running -> done
        running -> abandoned
        terminal: done, abandoned, cancelled
    }

    is_ready: prerequisites.all(p => p.status = done)
    node_available: node.status = complete and node.reproducible and node.payload?.tier in {hot, cold}
    node_failed: node.status = failed or not node.reproducible
    request_failed: request.status = unfulfilled
}

-- The memoized calculation itself. Immutable once complete; shared by every
-- step, in any request, that resolves to the same calc_id.
entity CalculationNode {
    calc_id: String
    model: ModelVersion
    as_of: Timestamp
    inputs: Set<data/InputVersion>
    prerequisite_nodes: Set<CalculationNode>
    data_class: data/DataClass
    created_at: Timestamp
    attempts: Integer
    dispatched_at: Timestamp?
    status: pending | computing | complete | failed

    result_hash: String when status = complete
    completed_at: Timestamp when status = complete
    failure_reason: String when status = failed
    failed_at: Timestamp when status = failed

    transitions status {
        pending -> computing
        computing -> complete
        computing -> pending
        computing -> failed
        terminal: complete, failed
    }

    -- False once a recomputation failed to reproduce result_hash.
    reproducible: Boolean

    payload: ret/ResultPayload with node = this
    steps: PlannedStep with node = this

    -- Rerun recipe (D-064): the node's identity (model code_hash, as_of, input
    -- versions, prerequisite result hashes, all under a named hash scheme)
    -- plus the environment of the attempt that produced the result. With the
    -- attempt log it is kept as long as the node.
    attempt_log: insp/ComputationAttempt with node = this
    computed_by: insp/ComputationAttempt when status = complete
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    max_attempts: Integer = 3
    compute_timeout: Duration = 10.minutes
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Model governance (D-016): models are registered when a release activates;
-- there is no runtime submission or approval. Independent validation of each
-- model version, and the gate it puts on production releases, are spec 21
-- (D-050).
rule RegisterReleaseModels {
    when: cfg/ReleaseActivated(release, previous)
    ensures:
        for m in release_models(release):
            if not exists ModelVersion{code_hash: model_code_hash(m)}:
                ModelVersion.created(
                    function_name: model_function_name(m),
                    version: model_version_label(m),
                    code_hash: model_code_hash(m),
                    first_release: release,
                    registered_at: now
                )
}

-- A request is pinned to a knowledge time: now by default, or an earlier
-- instant (batch cut-off D-016, or reproducing what was known in the past).
-- Its subjects must resolve at (as_of, known_at), otherwise it is refused up
-- front (D-008). Production data needs a model in the active production
-- release; golden, synthetic and test may also use the active development
-- release. A whatif request (D-038) uses production models and may name
-- production subjects alongside its author's whatif series.
rule AcceptCalculationRequest {
    -- model: calc_graph.submit
    when: CalculationRequested(requester, model, subjects, data_class, as_of, known_at?, origin_ref?)
    let pinned = known_at ?? now
    requires: pinned <= now
    requires: data/permitted(requester, "calc.request", model)
    requires: subjects.all(s => s.data_class = data_class
        or (data_class = whatif and s.data_class = production))
    requires: subjects.all(s => s.data_class != whatif or s.created_by = requester)
    requires: subjects.all(s => resolvable(s, as_of, pinned))
    requires: model_in_release(model, cfg/active_release(production))
        or (data_class in {golden, synthetic, test} and model_in_release(model, cfg/active_release(development)))
    ensures: CalculationRequest.created(
        requested_by: requester,
        model: model,
        subjects: subjects,
        pinned_inputs: {},
        data_class: data_class,
        as_of: as_of,
        known_at: pinned,
        origin_ref: origin_ref,
        requested_at: now,
        status: open,
        plan_expanded: false
    )
}

-- Planning is itself a memoized Julia calculation (D-018, D-020): its calc_id
-- covers planner, subjects' content and release, so the same plan is reused.
rule StartPlanning {
    when: request: CalculationRequest.created
    ensures: PlannedStep.created(
        request: request,
        model: planner_for(request.model),
        inputs: resolve_all(request.subjects, request.as_of, request.known_at)
            + request.pinned_inputs,
        prerequisites: {},
        purpose: planning,
        status: waiting
    )
}

-- One pass (D-020): the plan names every evaluation node with the series it
-- needs and its prerequisites. Elixir resolves the series bitemporally; it
-- never interprets instrument content. Any unresolvable series fails the
-- request before evaluation starts (D-008).
rule ExpandPlan {
    when: step: PlannedStep.status transitions_to done
    requires: step.status = done
    requires: step.purpose = planning
    requires: step.request.status = open
    requires: not step.request.plan_expanded
    let request = step.request
    let entries = plan_entries(step.node)
    ensures:
        if unresolved_series(entries, request.as_of, request.known_at).count > 0:
            PlanningFailed(request: request, reason: "unresolvable input series")
        else:
            request.plan_expanded = true
            for e in entries:
                StepPlanned(
                    request: request,
                    model: entry_model(e),
                    inputs: resolve_all(entry_series(e), request.as_of, request.known_at),
                    prerequisites: entry_prerequisites(e, request)
                )
}

rule AddPlannedStep {
    when: StepPlanned(request, model, inputs, prerequisites)
    requires: request.status = open
    requires: prerequisites.all(p => p.request = request)
    requires: inputs.all(v => v.series.data_class = request.data_class
        or (request.data_class in {whatif, impact} and v.series.data_class = production))
    ensures: PlannedStep.created(
        request: request,
        model: model,
        inputs: inputs,
        prerequisites: prerequisites,
        purpose: evaluation,
        status: waiting
    )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Requester {
    identified_by: data/User
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface CalculationRequests {
    facing requester: Requester
    context request: CalculationRequest where requested_by = requester

    exposes:
        request.status
        request.as_of
        request.known_at
        for s in request.steps:
            s.status

    provides:
        CalculationRequested(requester, model, subjects, data_class, as_of, known_at?, origin_ref?)
        RequestCancellationRequested(requester, request) when request.status = open
}

surface ModelCatalogue {
    facing requester: Requester
    context model: ModelVersion

    exposes:
        model.function_name
        model.version
        model.code_hash
        model.first_release.tag
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deferred Specifications
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Black boxes owned by Julia (D-018), read by Elixir only as opaque data:
--   planner_for(model)          planner model version for a function, from the
--                               tagged configuration release
--   plan_entries(node)          the DAG in a completed planning node's payload
--   entry_model / entry_series / entry_prerequisites(e, request)
--                               fields of one DAG entry; prerequisites map the
--                               plan's local ids to this request's steps
-- Black boxes owned by Elixir:
--   bounded(text, limit): the text cut to `limit` bytes (null stays null).
--   resolvable, resolve_all, unresolved_series (as_of, known_at): via data/resolve_version.
--   calc_id_of(...), prerequisite_result_hashes(...), prerequisite_nodes(...): a
--   canonical, collision-resistant hash of hashes (D-018).
--   node_class_of(step): the request's class, except in a whatif request
--   where a step with no whatif input and no whatif prerequisite is
--   production (D-038); in an impact request every step is impact (D-067).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved: canonical rounding (D-004, D-015), up-front failure (D-008),
-- development retention 7 days hot then 30 days cold, 37 days in total (D-005).
open question "Is the stored payload itself canonically rounded, or kept at full precision with only result_hash computed on the rounded form?"open question "Provenance record: exactly which fields must an auditor see for a reported figure (requester, known_at, model code_hash, every input version, worker build)?"
-- Resolved (D-064): the provenance fields for a node are its identity, the environment of the attempt that produced it (build, Julia, release, threads, reduction order) and a log of every attempt; requester and known_at stay with the request.
open question "A failed node is terminal for its calc_id. Should an operator be able to reset an infrastructure-failed node, given calc_id identity? Related (D-064): a failed node has no payload, so it can be inspected through its attempt log but not rerun; should a diagnostic rerun of a failed node be allowed, with its output kept outside the ledger?"
-- Resolved (D-064, item 7): the environment is deliberately outside calc_id, so a rerun in another environment must give the same hash (Determinism). Another hash is a breach whatever the environment, and the node is marked irreproducible; environment_differs only tells the reviewer which side to suspect first.
open question "Worker capacity and priority: how are dispatched nodes ordered when the bounded queue is full (e.g. regulatory EOD runs first)?"
-- Resolved (D-062), found by the calculation-graph model (models/calc_graph.qnt, D-061): a purged payload is recomputed once however many steps wait for it (the payload carries a recomputing flag), and a step binding to an irreproducible node is abandoned at binding.
-- Resolved (D-063): a lost recomputation is retried with its own attempt count (same cap), and when attempts run out the waiting steps are abandoned while the node stays reproducible; a later step starts a fresh cycle (RecomputationCrashes).
-- Resolved (D-071): spec 07 carries recomputation tickets (JobTicket.purpose): 07d EnqueueRecomputation, AssignJob, BundleCommitted and WorkerDies emit RecomputationAssigned, RematerialisationCompleted and RecomputationCrashed for them. The dispatch model treats a ticket alike whatever its purpose.
